.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CODEX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm codex
.Nd OpenAI's coding agent for the terminal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar prompt
.Nm
.Cm exec
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar prompt
.Nm
.Cm login
.Op Fl -device-auth | Fl -with-api-key
.Nm
.Cm login status
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is OpenAI's Codex: an agent that reads and changes the files of a
directory and runs commands there, at the request of a person or of a
model it talks to on OpenAI's service.
With no command it draws its screen and takes requests one after another;
.Cm exec
runs one request without the screen and prints the result.
.Ql codex --help
lists the commands, and
.Ql codex Ar command Fl -help
describes one.
.Pp
The x86-64 GNU root has
.Nm
when it is built with the agents layer:
.Ql make ARCH=x86_64 GNU_AGENTS=codex os-real-gnu-ext2 ,
or
.Ql GNU_AGENTS=all ,
which the release to the installed machine uses.
The program is OpenAI's own build of version 0.162.1 for
x86_64-unknown-linux-musl, a static executable, installed unmodified in
.Pa /opt/codex
with the helpers its package has:
.Pa bin/codex-code-mode-host ,
ripgrep as
.Pa codex-path/rg ,
bubblewrap as
.Pa codex-resources/bwrap ,
zsh as
.Pa codex-resources/zsh/bin/zsh ,
and the voice host with its libraries in
.Pa codex-resources/voice .
.Pa /usr/bin/codex
and
.Pa /bin/codex
link to
.Pa /opt/codex/bin/codex .
OpenAI's license, Apache-2.0, and its notice are
.Pa /opt/codex/LICENSE
and
.Pa /opt/codex/NOTICE ;
the licenses of ripgrep, bubblewrap and zsh are in
.Pa /opt/codex/licenses ,
and those of the voice libraries in
.Pa /opt/codex/codex-resources/voice/licenses .
.Ss Signing in over SSH
The screen first offers three ways to sign in.
.Dq Sign in with ChatGPT
prints an address to open in a web browser and waits for the browser to
return to a server that
.Nm
runs on port 1455 of this machine's loopback interface.
Over SSH, either forward that port from the computer that has the browser,
with
.Ql ssh -L 1455:localhost:1455 ,
or choose
.Dq Sign in with Device Code ,
which
.Ql codex login --device-auth
also starts:
.Nm
prints an address and a one-time code, and the code is entered at that
address on any other computer, by a ChatGPT account whose security settings
allow sign-in by a device code.
.Pp
.Dq Provide your own API key ,
or
.Ql codex login --with-api-key ,
which reads the key from standard input, signs in with an OpenAI API key
instead, for which OpenAI bills by use:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printenv OPENAI_API_KEY | codex login --with-api-key
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql codex login status
says how
.Nm
is signed in, and
.Ql codex logout
removes the credentials.
.Ss Where the credentials live
.Nm
keeps the credentials of a sign-in in plain text in
.Pa ~/.codex/auth.json ,
with mode 0600, which it reads at each start; treat the file as a
password.
.Ev CODEX_HOME
names another directory in place of
.Pa ~/.codex .
The setting
.Ql cli_auth_credentials_store
can name a keyring instead, which Rach2 does not have.
.Ss Settings
.Nm
reads its settings from
.Pa /etc/codex/config.toml ,
then from
.Pa ~/.codex/config.toml ,
whose settings take the place of the first's, and then from
.Fl c Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
on the command line.
Rach2's
.Pa /etc/codex/config.toml
makes three settings:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql check_for_update_on_startup = false
.Nm
does not ask GitHub for its latest release when its screen starts, and
offers no update.
.Nm
does not replace itself here in any case: it counts
.Pa /opt/codex
as a local build, which it does not update, and a new version comes with a
new build of the root.
.It Ql features.daemon_auto_start = false
.Nm
runs the sessions of its screen in its own process, as
.Fl -no-daemon
has it do.
With the setting on, it copies its whole package, some 430 MB, to
.Pa ~/.codex/packages/app-server-daemon
and starts two processes from the copy that outlive it: the shared
server that runs the sessions, and an updater that installs OpenAI's newer
releases in that directory.
.Ql codex agents ,
which lists the sessions of that server, needs it.
.It Ql sandbox_mode = \(dqdanger-full-access\(dq
The commands that the model asks for run without Codex's sandbox.
On Linux the sandbox runs each command under the bundled bubblewrap, which
needs kernel features that Rach2 does not have yet, so a command run in it
fails.
Without it a command has every right of the user who runs
.Nm ,
so run
.Nm
as an ordinary user, on files that the work can afford to change.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /opt/codex/bin/codex
the program
.It Pa /opt/codex/LICENSE
its license
.It Pa /etc/codex/config.toml
Rach2's settings
.It Pa ~/.codex/config.toml
a person's settings
.It Pa ~/.codex/auth.json
the credentials of a sign-in
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success and 1 on an error.
.Ql codex login status
exits 1 when no account is signed in.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Sign in from an SSH session, then ask for one change in the current
directory without the screen:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ codex login --device-auth
$ codex exec "Fix the failing test in tests/parse.sh"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gh 1 ,
.Xr git 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr tmux 1
.Pp
.Lk https://developers.openai.com/codex "The Codex documentation"
